   266	      Playing the Game                                            adventurers are talking to a noble, exploring a ruin,
   267	                                                                  or fighting a dragon. In certain situations—partic-
   268	                                                                  ularly combat—the action is more structured, and
   269	    Rhythm of Play                                                everyone takes turns.
   270	    The three main pillars of D&D play are social in-
   271	    teraction, exploration, and combat. Whichever one
   272	                                                                  The Six Abilities
   273	    you’re experiencing, the game unfolds according to            All creatures—characters and monsters—have six
   274	    this basic pattern:                                           abilities that measure physical and mental charac-
   275	                                                                  teristics, as shown on the Ability Descriptions table.
   276	    1: The Game Master Describes a Scene. The GM
